---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url.js';
import { TAX_YEAR } from '../../lib/tax.js';

const faqGroups = [
  {
    group: 'Tax & Thresholds',
    items: [
      {
        q: 'My pay went up but it feels like I lost more of it to tax — what\'s going on?',
        a: `That's fiscal drag at work. The £12,570 Personal Allowance and the £50,270 Higher Rate threshold have stayed put since 2021/22, and the Budget in November 2025 pushed that freeze out to April 2031. Salaries don't stand still the same way — so a pay rise that only keeps pace with inflation still moves more of your income into taxed territory, or across the boundary into the 40% band, purely because the goalposts haven't moved. No rate has gone up; the tax take rises anyway.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'I just crossed £100,000 — why did my take-home pay barely move (or fall)?',
        a: `Between £100,000 and £125,140 of "adjusted net income", you don't just pay 40% Income Tax — you also lose £1 of your £12,570 Personal Allowance for every £2 you earn, until it's gone entirely at £125,140. Stack the two together and the effective rate on that slice of pay lands near 60%. A £1,000 rise inside that band can shrink to roughly £400 after deductions. Pension contributions or Gift Aid that bring your adjusted net income back under £100,000 are the usual way people sidestep this band.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Scottish tax keeps diverging from the rest of the UK — how far apart are they now?',
        a: `Since 2017 Holyrood has set its own Income Tax rates, and the gap has widened every year. Scotland now runs six bands — Starter 19%, Basic 20%, Intermediate 21%, Higher 42%, Advanced 45%, Top 48% — against three bands (20/40/45) for England, Wales and Northern Ireland. This year the Scottish Basic and Intermediate thresholds jumped 7.4%, comfortably ahead of inflation, while Higher, Advanced and Top stayed frozen. The Advanced band was also stretched so 45% now runs all the way to £125,140, lining up with where the rest-of-UK Additional rate begins. National Insurance doesn't change with postcode — that stays identical across the whole UK.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Employer NI went up — does that make Salary Sacrifice less attractive for me?',
        a: `The opposite, if anything. Employers now pay 15% National Insurance (up from 13.8%) on pay above just £5,000 a year, down from a £9,100 threshold — both changes have applied since April 2025 and carry through unchanged. Salary you sacrifice, typically into a pension, still escapes your Income Tax and your 8%/2% employee NI, and now escapes a bigger 15% employer NI charge too. Because employers save more per pound sacrificed than before, many choose to funnel some of that extra saving into your pension rather than keep it, which is why sacrifice schemes have generally become more generous, not less.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Is it true bonuses get hit with a special, higher tax rate?',
        a: `No — that's a payslip illusion, not a real rule. A bonus is just added to that period's pay and run through the same PAYE system as your normal salary. Because PAYE assumes each month looks like a twelfth of your annual pay, a big bonus in one month can temporarily look like a much higher annual salary, pushing that month into a steeper band and making the deduction seem huge. HMRC's cumulative PAYE calculation sorts this out automatically over the rest of the tax year — the extra tax withheld comes back to you through smaller deductions later, or as a refund.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'A colleague earns exactly what I do but takes home more — why?',
        a: `Same gross salary doesn't guarantee the same net pay. Differences usually trace back to: the tax code each of you has been issued (shaped by benefits in kind, a second job, or HMRC recovering an old underpayment), whether one of you is a Scottish taxpayer, whether either of you carries a student loan and on which plan, and how your pension contributions are structured — salary sacrifice lowers your taxable pay in a way that a standard "relief at source" pension doesn't. Any of these on their own can produce a noticeably different take-home figure from an identical headline salary.`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: 'Student Loans & Benefits',
    items: [
      {
        q: `Which Student Loan threshold applies to me this year?`,
        a: `It depends on your plan. For ${TAX_YEAR}: Plan 1 starts repayments at £26,900, Plan 2 at £29,385, Plan 4 (Scotland) at £33,795, Plan 5 at £25,000, and the Postgraduate Loan at £21,000, a figure that hasn't moved since it launched. Above your threshold you repay 9% of the excess (6% on a Postgraduate Loan — 15% combined if you're repaying both an undergraduate plan and a Postgraduate Loan at once). Worth flagging: the Plan 2 threshold has just risen to £29,385, but the government has already announced it will be locked at that level from April 2027 through to April 2030.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'Did Child Benefit really switch to a household income test this year?',
        a: `No — that change was floated in 2024 for an April 2026 launch, then quietly dropped at the Autumn 2024 Budget. The High Income Child Benefit Charge still looks only at the higher earner's individual adjusted net income, not the household's combined total. It kicks in at £60,000, claws back 1% of Child Benefit for every £200 earned above that, and wipes it out completely at £80,000. So the old quirk persists: two parents each earning £59,000 keep every penny of Child Benefit, while one parent alone on £80,000+ gets none — nothing about that has changed for ${TAX_YEAR}.`,
      },
      {
        q: 'How can a tiny pay rise leave me worse off overall?',
        a: `It's rarely the Income Tax bands themselves — it's a benefit or allowance that disappears in one lump rather than tapering gradually. Nudging past £60,000 starts the Child Benefit clawback, and a jump from, say, £79,500 to £80,500 can erase what's left of it in a single step. Crossing £100,000 starts eroding your Personal Allowance and, for parents, ends eligibility for free childcare hours and Tax-Free Childcare outright rather than reducing it bit by bit. A small increase in gross pay near any of these lines can cost you far more in lost support than the raise itself is worth.`,
      },
    ],
  },
];

const keyFigures = [
  { label: 'Personal Allowance', value: '£12,570' },
  { label: 'Basic Rate', value: '20%' },
  { label: 'Higher Rate Threshold', value: '£50,270' },
  { label: 'Higher Rate', value: '40%' },
];
---
<Layout
  title={`UK Tax FAQs ${TAX_YEAR} — Fiscal Drag, £100k Trap, Student Loans | salary-calc.co.uk`}
  description={`Clear, sourced answers on UK Income Tax, National Insurance and take-home pay for the ${TAX_YEAR} tax year — fiscal drag, the £100,000 trap, Scottish tax, salary sacrifice, student loans and Child Benefit.`}
>
  <main class="container py-4 pb-5">
    <p class="small text-muted mb-2"><a href={withBase()}>Home</a> &gt; Frequently Asked Questions</p>
    <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:65ch;">Clear answers on UK tax, National Insurance, and take-home pay for the {TAX_YEAR} tax year.</p>

    <h2 class="h6 fw-bold text-uppercase mt-4 mb-3" style="color:#505a5f; letter-spacing:0.03em;">Key {TAX_YEAR.slice(0, 4)} tax figures (UK)</h2>
    <div class="row g-3 mb-5">
      {keyFigures.map((f) => (
        <div class="col-6 col-md-3">
          <div class="stat-box h-100">
            <div class="small text-muted">{f.label}</div>
            <div class="fw-bold" style="font-size:1.5rem;">{f.value}</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    {faqGroups.map((section) => (
      <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="h4 fw-bold mb-3" style="color:var(--govblack);">{section.group}</h2>
        {section.items.map((item) => (
          <details class="mb-3 pb-3" style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--govborder);">
            <summary class="h6 mb-0">{item.q}</summary>
            <p class="mt-2 mb-0" style="max-width:70ch;">{item.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </section>
    ))}

    <div class="zero-note">
      <p class="small mb-1"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.gov.uk/income-tax-rates" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HMRC Income Tax rates</a>, <a href="https://www.gov.uk/scottish-income-tax" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GOV.UK Scottish Income Tax</a>, <a href="https://www.gov.uk/repaying-your-student-loan/what-you-pay" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GOV.UK Student Loans</a>, and House of Commons Library research briefings on the High Income Child Benefit Charge and employer National Insurance.</p>
      <p class="small mb-0">This page is for general guidance only and does not constitute financial or tax advice. Figures are correct for the {TAX_YEAR} tax year as published by HMRC and the Scottish Government; always check <a href="https://www.gov.uk/income-tax-rates" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gov.uk</a> for the latest position before making decisions.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
